    [
     2,
     "On 2012-Mar-19, at 14:37 , Heike Hofmann wrote:<br>&gt; <br>&gt; you can use qplot for showing multiple time series - the easiest way would be to melt the variables into a single variable and then plot:<br>&gt; <br>&gt; X1 &lt;- rnorm(100)<br>&gt; X2 &lt;- rnorm(mean=3, 100)<br>&gt; X3 &lt;- rnorm(mean=1, 100)<br>&gt; frame &lt;- data.frame(X1=X1, X2=X2, X3=X3)<br>&gt; <br>&gt; <br>&gt; library(ggplot2)<br>&gt; frame$id &lt;- 1:nrow(frame)<br>&gt; framem &lt;- melt(frame, measure.vars=1:3)<br>&gt; qplot(id, value, group=variable, geom=&quot;line&quot;, colour=variable, data=framem)"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<p>That&#39;s fine if your series have the same units, but that does not seems to be the case in your example data frame. In that situation, consider using facets (facet_grid() or facet_wrap()) with the argument scales=&quot;free_y&quot;.<p>Jean-Olivier Irisson<br>---<br>Observatoire Océanologique<br>Station Zoologique, B.P. 28, Chemin du Lazaret<br>06230 Villefranche-sur-Mer<br>Tel: <a href=\"tel:+33%204%2093%2076%2038%2004\" value=\"+33493763804\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+33 04 93 76 38 04</a><br>Mob: <a href=\"tel:+33%206%2021%2005%2019%2090\" value=\"+33621051990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+33 06 21 05 19 90</a><br><a href=\"http://jo.irisson.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.com/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792376533406000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2qBO71teZlR-_DE9HYaPDT\">http://jo.irisson.com/</a><p></p></p></p>"
